        "summary": "The Strait of Hormuz remains severely disrupted. PortWatch recorded 8 transits on 2026-08-08, the most recent published day under the weekly Tuesday release cycle, against a pre-crisis baseline of 73 per day. Separately, our AIS-derived count shows 403 vessels holding position away from berth as of 11:53 UTC, a figure that excludes ships within 25 km of a working port. Port arrivals across the Hormuz-area ports on both the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman sides reached 842 vessels in the most recent 24 hours, measured under a different methodology and not comparable to the PortWatch transit figure. The Crisis Pressure composite sits in the extreme band, flat over the past 24 hours, with physical transit deviation the top contributor. The Escalation Forecast composite is in the elevated band and rising, driven by Polymarket escalation contracts; the two composites diverge, with state conditions stable while the forward signal worsens. Brent stood at $88.90, up 0.53% over 24 hours, as of 11:53 UTC. On the diplomatic track, per Iranian state media, Iran's Supreme National Security Council chief has stated that full reopening of the strait is conditional on United States acceptance of Iran's terms, a claim originating with Mehr News that has not been independently confirmed. Unverified reports carried by theuknews.com cite six fatalities in a Red Sea shipping attack; no independent source has corroborated the casualty figure. Seventeen events were indexed in the past 24 hours.",
